#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:31
#: d5afe90c1f4744cfbca93f588ef9b46f
msgid "It's easy to create a powerful \"hardware in the loop\" simulator with APM, with a Flight Simulator \"fooling\" APM with sensor and GPS data like it would experience in flight, and then APM flying the plane accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:36
#: 39ea2715c3a542f1a667b6c925e46d56
msgid "The overall setup looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:42
#: 4a330ac990b0435d9a72809d313b282f
msgid "Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:44
#: 5c5cae670e5d4b84975b7f20cc1648dc
msgid "Download the `X-Plane 10 flight simulator <http://www.x-plane.com/desktop/home/>`__, if you don't already have it. The demo is free but times out after ten minutes. It's well worth `buying the full version <http://www.x-plane.com/store/landing/>`__, which is just $29.99. We support the 10.x versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:48
#: e22b44716b9c445a94d60a66a251b001
msgid "Load the HIL Simulations version of the APM software with the Mission Planner (circled in red below; pick the one for the aircraft you want to simulate)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:51
#: 090639aad01748fbbd409027ccf0711d
msgid "If you haven't done a setup already, press \"APM Setup\" on the Mission Planner Firmware page, which opens another dialog. Follow the instructions on the \"Reset\", \"Radio Input\" and \"Mode\" tabs of the dialog. Then you're ready to sim!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:60
#: fff60ede824a447bb3f0e7064e13bf45
msgid "Connecting APM and X-Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:62
#: a71b4798236d479288c03147e6040440
msgid "The Mission Planner will serve as your bridge to X-plane and will send the output from your APM's serial port connection to the flight simulator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:66
#: 5c73205524104736b02c6ffa70a0523c
msgid "NOTE: If you're using a Mac, you can use a Perl-based solution (this also works on Windows, if you prefer Perl). Here's what you need: `X-Plane.pl <http://api.ning.com/files/xRNyASq2LjL3D2W7-D-4tvKSdm-2NifDygSAVsFxnngOUoIC4XkcPnGyPL-zeJJRNrWyuifxCVSQnyMVibddCBg-OzRzLOQ9/XPlane.pl>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:71
#: cd60f4f57d8045ba89d411d8f3969e36
msgid "X-Plane Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:73
#: 0684e85fc88540a78065c081ca0a4dd5
msgid "X-Plane communicates over a network interface to the Flight Planner. Because of this you can run X-Plane and the Flight Planner on different computers. This is helpful if X-Plane running on a slower computer. Frame rate is very important."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:78
#: 2d8f91e835b346cf9816e74535606475
msgid "You can choose any aircraft in X-Plane, but the most realistic one for our purposes is the PT-60 RC airplane. These are the screen shots that show the necessary settings you need to set in the Settings Menu – Data Input and Output:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:89
#: eea0001f98064032a9117c3fffeea77d
msgid "You may also want to turn on the Frame Rate to display on the screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:91
#: 5c9cdb61b3b0475ea8c756372cd7ad68
msgid "On the IP screen on Xplane, change the UDP data port to 49005 as shown below. (The Mission Planner also has an advanced IP setting dialog, but leave it at the default of 49000. 49005 is the UDP port coming in from Xplane, 49000 is the UDP port going out from the Mission Planner.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:99
#: 2f410b8a2242456ab82f68da2a110a67
msgid "If you are running X-plane on the same computer as APM Mission Planner, then enter the loopback IP address (127.0.0.1) as shown above. If you are running X-plane on a different computer, enter the IP address of the other computer here(for example 192.168.1.1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:105
#: 26179db2e5534514b4049a65b4fbff25
msgid "Flight planner settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:107
#: a6c21e35b4b044528da4ac2dcd1ec958
msgid "Now you're ready to run the simulation. Start the Mission Planner, selecting the COM port APM is assigned to and 115200 baud as the speed. Click on the Simulation tab and you will be taken to this screen:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:114
#: 28cc54a7f1644839a24829ab88466b6a
msgid "If you are using X-Plane 10.x, you must check the \"XPlane 10\" checkbox shown here:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:120
#: 6a04d9f72ef641df86ea581a92888948
msgid "If you are running APM Mission Planner on the same computer as X-Plane, then the default IP settings should be fine. But if you're in doubt, you can check the \"Advanced IP Settings\". The IP address should be 127.0.0.1 and the port should be 49000, as shown. If you are running X-plane on a different computer, though, enter the IP address of the other computer here (for example 192.168.1.2). The port should be 49000 in either case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:127
#: 2197ca98c00a4e8991b34b539d145b18
msgid "Once you've selected Sim Link Start/Stop, if APM is connected and Xplane is running, you should see the Mission Planner Sim screen fill with data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:131
#: cbaca38d384848108fdf8d5644b30a64
msgid "Turn your transmitter on and check your link to your receiver. (Remember that if you're using APM 1 you must plug an ESC and LiPo into the RC pins of APM to power the RC receiver; this is not necessary on APM 2, where the radio is powered by the USB). When you move the sticks you should see the APM outputs change on the Flight Planner Simulator window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:138
#: 333c3dc09fee4dbc942db1e351ab5ca3
msgid "For first testing, set your three RC toggle switch modes to Manual, Stabilize and RTL. You can do that via the Mission Planner's MAVLink setup. Hit control-G in the Mission Planner once you're connect to go to the setup screen, where you will find the Modes setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:144
#: 57a74b63b78d4bdb8a215747d8cd284b
msgid "Flight test"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:146
#: 50c8c2aada234414ab447a490bcfcea5
msgid "Now fly the plane in the sim with your RC transmitter. First, hit the \"C\" key so you can see your plane up close and from the side. Try each stick on your RC transmitter and make sure that the control surfaces go in the right direction in manual mode. If they don't, reverse them at your RC transmitter as you would with a normal RC airplane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:152
#: c0c4404dc7c74d0f84150ed500edb46c
msgid "You can now try a flight. Reload the aircraft in Xplane, hit \"Shift-8\" so you can see it from behind, and hit \"b\" to release the brakes. The aircraft should begin moving forward. You can steer it on the ground with your rudder stick. Once it's picked up speed, give it a little up elevator to take off. You should be able to fly it like a RC flight simulator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:159
#: 3e3d336f4564482a920a287da3677bd6
msgid "If it's flying well, try moving your toggle switch into stabilize mode. The aircraft should immediately start flying flat and level. If it rolls upside down and wants to fly that way, you'll need to reverse the ailerons on the SIM model on your transmitter. Likewise for diving."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:164
#: 0cba1bd13b8a4fb8b9258695b09f4593
msgid "If stabilization works well, try RTL. The aircraft should return to the start of the runway and circle there at 100m."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:167
#: 6228b9d38b3649e7a778f0c376ddb433
msgid "Once that is working, trying entering some waypoints and flying an auto mission. In the Mission Planner read the stored waypoints and let it reset home to the stored position (which will now be the airport in your Xplane simulator that you're flying from). Click to add a few waypoints and switch into auto to watch the show!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:173
#: ae3d7dcd671b4be4af941d7dcffe445d
msgid "In general, the correct order to load and run everything is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:175
#: 091de0433af44612b52b2766ec5bf848
msgid "Use the Mission Planner to load the Simulator version of the APM code and perform the setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:177
#: 94da4a09f3064c17bcea17324753110c
msgid "Start Xplane. After it is initialized and while the plane is sitting on the ground hit the \"a\" key for a rear view and then the \"p\" key to pause."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:180
#: 2e56cfdb971a46878f39f9c8f9efee7c
msgid "Start the Mission Planner, select the right port and baud for your APM board and connect to APM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:182
#: eb4ea69b25e24b3abb6c8c576f7d3fdf
msgid "In the Mission Planner simulation tab, click the button in ArduPilotSim to connect. You should see values appear in the output fields. Switch your TX mode switch to the manual position and verify that the outputs are moving with your TX sticks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:186
#: 48a6baa70e864b21a14827d8c3eef35a
msgid "Make sure your throttle stick is down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:187
#: 1c615e7f576f4090833bccdac329f29b
msgid "Switch to Xplane. You are ready to go. Click the \"p\" key to unpause. Hit the \"b\" key to unlock the brakes. Advance the throttle and take off!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:192
#: e4c05bb24d424270967bbba7d54872e1
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:194
#: 41b64c878ab34763a3ad6cdc1753784e
msgid "I have been using the stock PT-60 in Xplane with good results. I would recommend a cruise airspeed of 15 m/s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:196
#: a93a27b3a2244508b24212417397a867
msgid "One quirk of the PT-60 is that it bounces around a lot on the ground and if you are not careful it has prop-strikes followed by simulated engine failure, which is really annoying. If the engine has stopped, reload the plane. Hold the pitch stick for a modest climb and go to full throttle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:201
#: 05a47a4603ee4c82a5edfb118e548069
msgid "Remember that APM always sets its \"home\" location automatically at the field, which in this case is whichever airport you've set Xplane to start at. It will overwrite any home you may have set in the Mission Planner. If you want the flight sim to start at a different airport, you must select that from Xplane's \"Location\" menu. You can only select the provided airports, not just any place in the world you want to fly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:208
#: 4c6fafa39f5e4a60b1b3d2734d330eda
msgid "If you want to play with the code and load it via Arduino."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:210
#: c17a361c71a1462eb6ad868a7c3e635f
msgid ", rather than the pre-compiled hex file in the Mission Planner, you can. You just need to change your APM_config.h file to the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:218
#: 79abf3f188624c38b99e167fccc64459
msgid "HIL_MODE_SENSORS is not currently (Jan 2012) working. HIL_MODE_ATTITUDE informs Plane of the attitude of the plane but not the accelerations or roll rates. HIL_MODE_SENSORS originally was going to inject the lower level physics into the real sensor code, from which would (hopefully) be derived the same or similar attitude figures, plus more information from the Barometer and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:225
#: 399e6e299a374d39b50258f3fbd7837f
msgid "Software-in-the-loop (SITL) sim has taken this further, emulating !Arduino registers and injecting the sensor information at the lowest level possible. It is therefore the better option if you want to sim with the most realistic inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:231
#: 856b690d1ecb4f6984fa691321629d39
msgid "Error messages and fixes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:233
#: efc4ac5375d44f65bcdb0b454c93ea23
msgid "Can't open serial port : The com port has not been selected or the port is in use. Do you have a terminal open to the APM? Note that the mission planner loads the connection window coms port menu with every port it finds. If the USB driver failed to hook up and did not promote a port into !Windows, then this menu will be missing the actual port or may default to another port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:239
#: 48cffd4f80924595ac47071e57726559
msgid "Socket (IP) setup problem. Do you have this open already? : You have another program running that is using the IP Port and conflicting with the data communications between Xplanes and APM Planner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:242
#: 4a071a1f2c35436f872eeab17ccd434b
msgid "Xplane Data Problem - You need DATA IN/OUT 3, 18, 19, 20 : Please redo the setup and make sure all the boxes are ticked. You may need to restart X-Plane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:245
#: 258fa646cf214d18b71aecec50a8ca4d
msgid "Bad Gains!!! : One of the simulator gain numbers is invalid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:246
#: 8531d53f0c994bb1ac5480137c448ea7
msgid "NO SIM data : ArduPilot Mega Planner is not receiving any data from Xplane. Please check your Xplane settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:248
#: 4ff91cb65e874b5288657aec0d80c93d
msgid "The radio has no control--you push the sticks and nothing happens. Are your PID setting zero? Have you setup up your Radio inputs? The configuration defaults may not be appropriate for your radio. Please review the minimum, maximum and trim figures and if necessary use the radio Setup mode either from the mission planner or from the command line setup mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:254
#: d0f9f05f506e4f7783efc2c443555a75
msgid "Flight controls are way off. You need to push the sticks way over to fly the plane. Check your Radio setup in the APM. Did you configure the Radio? See (7)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:259
#: 55f8a95414c842a2a12cf7b98e509362
msgid "Debug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:261
#: f00cfaf361014bd7a05a779101644c15
msgid "The on-screen status details only update after a valid connection to the APM has been made."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:263
#: a9e434cc7ab641dab8183ad454061d07
msgid "Open Terminal, and verify what you see. If the text is readable go to the second point below, if it is not look at the first point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:266
#: 79d6c1aaf3a549c7980d4ac2f189c8b2
msgid "When you click Connect you should see the APM header printed in the text box. If you don't you may not have the correct Comport or Baudrate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:269
#: 55f41fb2b80c4ccaa18d5b2349517b9f
msgid "Verify the setup by opening the Terminal in APM Planner and see what it prints out. it should start with the APM header and continually spit out AAA???? forever. If you don't see the AAA's then you may have uploaded the wrong firmware (not simulation mode) or if you're loading the code manually, not made the necessary changes to the **APM_Config.h** file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:276
#: 50f9a1c33fcf4abaa07c825db3c413c6
msgid "Try re-doing the radio calibration after selecting the simulation, and make sure the throttle gain is set to 10,000 in the simulation tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:281
#: 405cab9d98d24b8c993a28cca9d536fc
msgid "Python Scripting with XPlane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:283
#: 21e4421713cc4224a37c2da0d1eff6a3
msgid "This section shows how to use Python for some basic scripted acrobatic moves for fixed-wing airplanes (including a left roll, a right roll and most of a loop). The instructions come from the tutorial `Scripted Fixed-Wing Acrobatics <https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/scripted-fixed-wing-acrobatics>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:287
#: 1d4659782de34891b75ea77fd1455223
msgid "You need a python based script. You can start with the one I've put together. It's attached here. \"`simple_script.txt <http://api.ning.com/files/e86-46o09Aysi4zyyv8mnDWV0ljIXbNUV*fTsE9MwLsZ8J1em1bwUNnHadjWSmygB*jnKvZwo7Bx0A*AHE8pZVGLkN9W4n1u/simple_script.txt>`__\\ \""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:290
#: 935e2a17477d4c238dfac9e4f622d942
msgid "With X-plane, the APM Mission Planner (MP) and the script open, get your plane in the air and press \"p\" for pause. \"a\" will put you in chase view (\"w\" to go back in the plane) and \"-\" and \"=\" will zoom your view of the plane in and out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:294
#: b9505aaafc554dea9276899623addadc
msgid "In the MP click on the \"Flight Data\" button, then select the \"Actions\" tab underneath the Heads-Up-Display."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:296
#: 6fe941f3576341c4b22c09fc006936b6
msgid "Click on the \"Script\" button. This will bring up another window with a preloaded script (for Copter). Switch to the already open \"simple_script.txt\" and select-all then copy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:299
#: 9267faf699ea42b1a2de8ad8f9f326bc
msgid "Go to the python script window and paste \"simple_script.txt\" in the python script window. DO NOT close it yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:301
#: dd15de025ff545e295f35761551cfe56
msgid "Go to X-plane, and press \"p\" again to un-pause the plane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:302
#: e67415293eac4c3abdd5e39511e2191c
msgid "Go back to your python script window and close it. This will trigger the execution of the script when you click \"yes\" in the pop-up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/x-plane-hardware-in-the-loop-simulation.rst:304
#: fc419b79dcec473fb521e1e045d53543
msgid "Watch your plane do some rolls, and most of a loop."
msgstr ""
